% M1-T23. The largest numbers lib/flatzinc/compile.ml will accept, driven through the
% whole CLI: parse, compile, propagate, branch, back off, solve, write the .opb, write
% the .pbp, verify.
%
% This is test_matrix.ml's int_lin_ne/common-factor-offset with its disequality
% multiplied through by K = 5 * 10^15, so the row reads 2K*e + 4K*g <> 16K. Divisibility
% is scale-invariant: at e = 2 the missing amount is 12K, which 4K divides, so g's lower
% bound is removed and g <= f closes that branch; at e = 3 the remainder is 2K and the
% disequality declines, which is how the solution survives. Identical reasoning to the
% unscaled model, seventeen digits wider.
%
% The row's magnitude -- |rhs| + sum |coeff| * max(|lo|, |hi|), the quantity the cap is
% stated over -- is 230000000000000000, 79.8% of Checked.limit. A disequality is the
% worst case that limit is sized against: Encoding.add_int_lin_ne folds the row's whole
% attainable span into the big-M coefficients of the A/B pair it posts, so the constants
% actually written to the .opb here are several times the row's own. That is envelope
% path 4 in lib/core/checked.ml's header, and this model is what measures it rather than
% assuming it.
%
% Before M1-T23 nothing in lib/ checked for overflow at all. The reason that was a
% soundness bug rather than a rejected proof is that the .opb row is expanded from the
% same arithmetic the propagator's slack uses, so a wrapped product corrupts both
% halves identically and veripb has nothing left to disagree with.
array [1..2] of int: dis = [10000000000000000, 20000000000000000];
var 1..5: e :: output_var;
var 1..5: f :: output_var;
var 1..5: g :: output_var;
constraint int_lin_ne(dis, [e, g], 80000000000000000);
constraint int_le(2, e);
constraint int_lin_eq([1, -1], [f, e], 1);
constraint int_le(3, g);
constraint int_le(g, f);
solve satisfy;
